                <pb facs="0117" n="17" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg17"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">863.00/868: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Austria</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kliefoth</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Vienna</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-02-16T13:00:00+01:00">February 16, 1934—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received February 16—11:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>23. Foreign Office complained bitterly over American correspondents’ despatches.
                    It is true that their reports must be read bearing in mind that <hi
                        rendition="#italic">New York Times</hi> correspondent, an Englishman, is an
                    ardent Socialist; that <hi rendition="#italic">New York Evening Post</hi>, a
                    Hungarian, also Socialist; that Associated Press violent pro-Nazi and
                    anti-American; and <hi rendition="#italic">Chicago Daily News</hi>, United Press
                    and International News are radicals. Unfortunately not one of our regular
                    American correspondents here. In view of their own political sympathies they are
                    hostile to Dollfuss for suppression of Socialists.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kliefoth</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
